# Sprint 15 — Controlled Adversary Simulation Platform

## Objective

Deploy a dedicated Kali Linux system for controlled adversary simulation within the Enterprise SOC Lab.

## System Deployed

| Host | Role | Network |
|---|---|---|
| KALI01 | Adversary Simulation | ATTACK-NET |

## Configuration

KALI01 was configured with:

- Kali Linux
- VMware guest integration
- Updated operating system packages
- Dedicated lab workspace
- Static network configuration
- Isolated virtual networking
- Defined testing scope and Rules of Engagement

## Network Configuration

KALI01:

- Address: 10.10.20.10/24
- VMware Network: VMnet3
- Segment: ATTACK-NET

VMnet3 has no VMware DHCP service and no host virtual adapter.

KALI01 currently has no route to the enterprise corporate environment.

## Isolation

The adversary platform was intentionally isolated from both the corporate network and external networks during initial deployment.

Controlled inter-network routing will be introduced through the firewall architecture in a later sprint.

## Adversary Simulation Workspace

The following directory structure was created:

soc-lab/
- notes/
- payloads/
- results/
- scripts/

## Security Tooling

Core utilities available on KALI01 were inventoried for later controlled testing.

## Rules of Engagement

Authorized testing boundaries were documented to ensure adversary simulations remain confined to systems owned and operated as part of the lab.

## Validation

The following were verified:

- Kali operating system functionality
- VMware guest integration
- Static ATTACK-NET address
- Network configuration persistence
- Corporate-network isolation
- External-network isolation

## Outcome

Sprint 15 established a dedicated and isolated adversary-simulation platform that will later generate controlled malicious behavior for SOC detection, investigation, and incident-response exercises.
